Do not leave the allocator-override hooks as weak external references.

BoringSSL declares OPENSSL_memory_alloc, OPENSSL_memory_free and
OPENSSL_memory_get_size as weak symbols on ELF so that an embedding
program can replace its allocator by defining them. In a shared object
that turns into three weak undefined entries in the dynamic symbol table.

This copy is linked statically into web-server modules, which are loaded
into a process they do not own. A module must not leave any cryptographic
symbol for the host process to resolve: whatever else is loaded there
could define these names and take over every allocation the module's
crypto makes. Nothing in this tree defines the hooks, so they are null in
every build today; this patch makes them null by construction, using the
static form BoringSSL already has for platforms without weak symbols.

Only the selection of that form changes. The hook call sites are untouched.

The macro this disables has one more user: the declaration of sdallocx,
compiled only when BORINGSSL_DETECT_SDALLOCX is defined. Nothing here
defines it, so nothing else changes today; a build that did define it
would get a null sdallocx (plain free) instead of a weak reference.

--- a/crypto/mem.cc
+++ b/crypto/mem.cc
@@ -58,7 +58,12 @@
 // EDK2 targets UEFI but builds as ELF and then translates the binary to
 // COFF(!). Thus it builds with __ELF__ defined but cannot actually cope with
 // weak symbols.
-#if !defined(__EDK2_BORINGSSL__) && defined(__ELF__) && defined(__GNUC__)
+//
+// mod_pagespeed: always take the static-null form below. This copy is linked
+// into server modules that are loaded into another program's process; a weak
+// reference would leave these names in the module's dynamic symbol table for
+// that process to satisfy.
+#if 0
 #define WEAK_SYMBOL_FUNC(rettype, name, args) \
   extern "C" {                                \
   rettype name args __attribute__((weak));    \
